`QueueBasedMessageHandler` captures a `Signposter` (`OSSignposter`) and an
`OSSignpostID` in explicitly `@Sendable` closures, which is an error in any
language mode unless those types are `Sendable`. Apple declared them so in a
later SDK than the one this toolchain is pinned to (`apple-sdk_14`, whose `os`
module is user-module-version 1049.100.4).

Upstream already handles exactly this for `OSSignpostIntervalState` with a
version-gated retroactive conformance; extend that block to cover the other two
types. The guard means this disappears by itself once the pinned SDK is new
enough, and upstream's `#if compiler(>=6.4)` reminder below it still applies.

--- a/Sources/SKLogging/Logging.swift
+++ b/Sources/SKLogging/Logging.swift
@@ -23,6 +23,8 @@
 // `OSSignpostIntervalState` to `Sendable`
 #if !canImport(os, _version: 1062.100)
 extension OSSignpostIntervalState: @retroactive @unchecked Sendable {}
+extension OSSignposter: @retroactive @unchecked Sendable {}
+extension OSSignpostID: @retroactive @unchecked Sendable {}
 #endif
 
 #if compiler(>=6.4)
